<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Władysław II of Opole</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1332–<a href="/years/1401">1401</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Władysław Opolczyk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Władysław II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a>, known as Opolczyk, was a Piast Duke of Opole in Silesia who served the Angevin kings as palatine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. In 1382 he founded the Pauline monastery of Jasna Góra at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czestochowa">Częstochowa</a> and brought there the Black Madonna icon, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s most revered image.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-of-opole.jpg" alt="Władysław II of Opole" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous (Poland)Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Poland_Foundation_of_the_Jasna_G%C3%B3ra_Monastery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
